<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE suite SYSTEM "https://testng.org/testng-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    PARALLEL CROSS-BROWSER suite (DEC-0014 / TASK-FWK-010).

    Runs the same scenarios on chrome, firefox and edge CONCURRENTLY, in one JVM, instead
    of three sequential Maven invocations.

        docker compose (profile grid) up -d
        ./mvnw test "-Dsurefire.suite=testng-crossbrowser.xml" "-Dexecution.mode=GRID" "-Denv=production"

    Intended for the GRID, where each browser gets its own container. It works locally too,
    but that needs all three browsers installed AND three real windows fighting for the
    desktop, so GRID is strongly preferred.

    NOTE ON THIS COMMENT: no double hyphen appears anywhere inside it. XML forbids that
    sequence in comments, and Surefire reports it as a bare SAXParseException naming a line
    number but not the reason. It has now bitten this repo twice (see testng-security.xml),
    which is why the docker flags above are written in words.

    HOW THE BROWSER REACHES THE DRIVER
    ==================================
    parallel="tests" gives each <test> block its own thread, and each passes a `browser`
    parameter. BaseRunner.pinBrowserForThread() reads it in @BeforeMethod and stores it in
    a THREAD-LOCAL that DriverFactory prefers over the JVM-wide -Dbrowser.

    That indirection is the whole point. The browser normally comes from a JVM-GLOBAL
    system property, so three <test> blocks writing it would race: last writer wins and all
    three would quietly run the SAME browser while this file claimed otherwise. Verified
    before building it that TestNG runs @BeforeMethod on the same thread as its @Test even
    under a parallel DataProvider and even when the pool reuses threads, which is what
    makes the thread-local correct rather than merely plausible.

    HOW TO CHECK IT ACTUALLY WORKED
    ===============================
    Do not trust a green run. The failure mode here is a SILENT WRONG ANSWER. Ask the Grid
    instead: each node serves only its own browser, so all three must report sessions.

        docker compose (profile grid) logs selenium-node-chrome  | grep -c "Session created"
        docker compose (profile grid) logs selenium-node-firefox | grep -c "Session created"
        docker compose (profile grid) logs selenium-node-edge    | grep -c "Session created"

    A zero on any of them means that browser never ran, whatever the report says. The
    Allure environment page should likewise list Browser.CHROME, Browser.FIREFOX and
    Browser.EDGE.

    THREAD BUDGET
    =============
    3 test blocks x data-provider-thread-count=2 gives up to 6 concurrent browsers, which
    exactly matches the Grid capacity (3 nodes x SE_NODE_MAX_SESSIONS=2 = 6 slots). Asking
    for more than the Grid can serve makes sessions queue and time out. Raise node REPLICAS
    if you want more throughput, not this number:
    a starved browser looks exactly like a flaky app.

    ROLLBACK
    ========
    Deleting this file fully disables the feature. The thread-local in DriverFactory goes
    inert because nothing sets it, and every other suite behaves exactly as it did before.
-->
<suite name="Timepieces Cross-Browser Suite" parallel="tests" data-provider-thread-count="2">

    <listeners>
        <listener class-name="com.timepieces.automation.listeners.MetricsListener" />
    </listeners>

    <test name="Chrome">
        <parameter name="environment" value="production" />
        <parameter name="browser" value="chrome" />
        <classes>
            <class name="com.timepieces.automation.bdd.runners.SmokeRunner" />
        </classes>
    </test>

    <test name="Firefox">
        <parameter name="environment" value="production" />
        <parameter name="browser" value="firefox" />
        <classes>
            <class name="com.timepieces.automation.bdd.runners.SmokeRunner" />
        </classes>
    </test>

    <test name="Edge">
        <parameter name="environment" value="production" />
        <parameter name="browser" value="edge" />
        <classes>
            <class name="com.timepieces.automation.bdd.runners.SmokeRunner" />
        </classes>
    </test>
</suite>
